A||||2||Fast Richard||'86 2.8 won't run when cold.||||5||
Z||000000||Fast Richard||06-03-2001||06:54 PM||||Our '86 SE, 2.8 just doesn't want to run when it's cold. The problem started suddenly. It's to the point of being undrivable until it warms up. The hesitation off idle is so bad that the engine bogs and the car bucks like a bronco (not the Ford kind). Here's what I've checked so far: EGR is working properly. MAP is new. Temp sensor for ECU is new. No vacuum leaks...and I've gone over it a dozen times! Other that the temp sensor, what tells the ECU to richen up the mix when cold? Any help would be greatly appreciated.||||reg||5
Z||000001||PBJ||06-03-2001||09:37 PM||||It sounds like you have a derivability problem before engine/ecm goes into closed loop.  The ecm takes over engine fuel delivery and timing after about 5-10 min of run time from a cold start.  So my thoughts are that maybe your distributor has moved and base timing is no longer correct. ??||||reg||1
Z||000002||Will||06-03-2001||09:41 PM||||I have exactly the same problem.<BR>My '87 GT is <I>really</I> cranky until it goes into closed loop mode.<BR>We have an Auto X-ray scanner, and have checked the following:<BR>Coolant temp reads correctly<BR>IAT reads correctly<BR>MAP seems slightly higher than it should be (vacuum leak? we haven't been able to find one).<BR>TPS is reading, although we haven't checked to see if it is slow or accurate.<BR>BLM is 128.<BR>The exhaust smells rich, and the injector pulse width is somewhat high and gradually goes down as it gets closer to going closed loop.<BR>It does get cross-counts once it goes closed loop. I just replaced the oxygen sensor a few weeks ago.<P>Any ideas?||||reg||1
